const title       = 'Are Ravens Monogamous?';
const description = 'Ravens are socially monogamous and usually maintain long-term pair bonds — but are not strictly genetically monogamous. This page examines raven mating systems, pair bond duration, divorce, and what "monogamy" means in raven biology.';
const path        = '/questions/are-ravens-monogamous/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Are Ravens Monogamous?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens stay with the same mate for life?', a: 'Ravens typically maintain long-term pair bonds that often last for many years, and sometimes for the bird\'s entire life — but "mate for life" in the strict sense overstates the case. Pair bonds can be dissolved ("divorce") if breeding repeatedly fails, and mates die, requiring finding a new partner. The question "do ravens mate for life?" is answered more fully at the linked page below, but the short answer is: ravens form long-term bonds, often lasting many years, but not invariably for life.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens really monogamous?', a: 'Ravens are socially monogamous — they maintain an exclusive pair bond and raise chicks together — but genetic studies of corvids (and birds generally) show extra-pair copulations occur in many supposedly monogamous species. Direct genetic paternity testing of raven broods is limited, but extra-pair paternity has been documented in closely related crows. Social monogamy (one social partner per breeding season) is consistent; strict genetic monogamy (no extra-pair mating) is likely not universal.' },
  { q: 'How do ravens choose their mates?', a: 'Mate choice in ravens involves a prolonged assessment period. Young ravens (typically 2–3 years old) in non-breeding flocks engage in courtship behaviours including aerial displays (tumbling, soaring together), vocalisation, and mutual preening. Mate quality indicators include territory quality, demonstrated foraging ability, body condition, and willingness to engage in allopreening and food-sharing. Once a pair bond forms, it is maintained through continued affiliative behaviours including allopreening, food-sharing, and coordinated territorial defence.' },
];
---

